<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prince Henry the Navigator</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1394">1394</a>–<a href="/years/1460">1460</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Prince Henry, Henry's · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aviz">House of Aviz</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Henry the Navigator was a fifteenth-century Portuguese prince, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-portugal">King João I</a>, who took part in the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> in 1415. He sponsored a series of expeditions down the West African coast and promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cultivation on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a>. His patronage turned exploration into a systematic enterprise and laid the foundations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator.jpg" alt="Prince Henry the Navigator" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nuno Gonçalves · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_the_Navigator1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was born on 4 March 1394, the third surviving son of King John I of Portugal, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aviz">House of Aviz</a>, and of Philippa, sister of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-england">Henry IV of England</a>. He may have been born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a>. As early as 1410 or 1412 he had commissioned captains to explore the coasts beyond Cape Non. In 1415, at 21, he took part in the capture of the Moorish port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>, where he was knighted with his brothers Edward and Peter and made Duke of Viseu, the first time the title duke was used in Portugal. He relieved Ceuta again in 1418, and the two expeditions let him gather intelligence about the lands to the south from Muslim prisoners.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1419 Henry became governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a>, and in 1420 administrator of the Order of Christ, which funded his plans. His brother King Edward granted him all profits from trade in areas he discovered and the sole right to authorise expeditions beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-bojador">Cape Bojador</a>. He was the main organiser of the disastrous expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> in 1437, after which his younger brother Ferdinand stayed a hostage until his death. Henry promoted the colonisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a> during Peter's regency.</p><p>Henry directed the development of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravel">caravel</a>, a lighter ship that could sail farther, and sponsored voyages, taking a 20 per cent tax on their profits. João Gonçalves Zarco and Tristão Vaz Teixeira found Porto Santo in 1418 and settlers reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a> in 1420. After fifteen failed expeditions in ten years, Gil Eanes passed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-bojador">Cape Bojador</a> in 1434. The Portuguese reached the Bay of Arguin in 1443 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal-river">Senegal River</a> in 1444. Gold and slaves began arriving in Portugal, and by 1452 the gold allowed the minting of the first cruzado coins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Henry died on 13 November 1460 and was buried in the Monastery of Batalha. No one called him Henry the Navigator in his lifetime or for three centuries: two nineteenth-century German historians coined the name, and British biographers popularised it. The idea of a school of navigators at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagres">Sagres</a> is regarded by modern historians as a misconception, created in the 18th century. He employed some cartographers, but there was no organised centre of navigation science. By 1462 the Portuguese had explored the African coast as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prince Henry the Navigator” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Diet of Schwäbisch Hall in 1190 Emperor Henry VI confirmed that the ducal rank applied to <b>Henry</b>'s own territories, which included Louvain, Brussels, Antwerp and lands stretching north towards the Meuse, rather than to the whole of Lower Lorraine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the patronage of <b>Prince Henry the Navigator</b> its sailors settled Madeira and the Azores, passed Cape Bojador in 1434, and pushed down the African coast until Bartolomeu Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope in 1488.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Baldwin's title closely copied that of the Byzantine emperors, and <b>Henry</b>'s letters used three versions, Emperor of Romania, of the Romans and of Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/empire/latin-empire">Latin Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Muslim port of Ceuta on the North African coast on 21 August 1415, led by King João I and his sons, including <b>Prince Henry</b>, later celebrated as “the Navigator”, began the imperial project, though Ceuta itself proved a commercial disappointment as Saharan caravan trade bypassed the captured city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The European colonial age is conventionally dated from 1415, when the army of John I of Portugal and his sons, among them <b>Prince Henry the Navigator</b>, took the Muslim port of Ceuta on the Moroccan coast.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The song belongs to the first years of <b>Henry</b>'s reign, a time of exuberance and extravagance at the English court, made possible by the kingdom's political stability and the wealth of the royal finances.</blockquote><a href="/songs/pastime-with-good-company">Pastime with Good Company</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in Sagres, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.15,-8.61&amp;year=1436" aria-label="Porto on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4340 696 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Prince Henry the Navigator"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4790 966h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4790 966h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.15,-8.61&amp;year=1436">What was here in 1436? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
